/**
 * BREAST CANCER (ALL TYPES): LIVING WITH IT, AND THE DECISIONS THAT ARE SHARED (UK and NHS focus).
 * Facts checked 25 September 2026.
 *
 * The family page is where a frightened person lands before anyone has told them which receptor pattern it is, and
 * this file is the part of the story that is true whichever result comes back. It carries seven decision rows the
 * decisions page turns into sections (breast-conserving surgery or mastectomy, with the long-term figures from the
 * trials rather than the phrase "similar outcomes"; reconstruction, delayed reconstruction or neither; surgery to
 * the armpit and the lymphoedema that lasts for life; fertility and early menopause, which has to be decided before
 * treatment starts; body image, sensation and sex; hair and scalp cooling; and follow-up, including which symptom is
 * worth a phone call). It carries the hand-written question set the appointment sheet, prep pack and decision pages
 * read (exported as `breastQuestions`, wired in src/data/questions.ts), six glossary terms, and supplements that
 * attach the breast-wide living-with records the triple-negative layer already owns to the family page rather than
 * writing them again.
 *
 * What is deliberately NOT here, because it belongs to a receptor subtype and the family page points at it:
 * chemotherapy before or after surgery and what pembrolizumab adds (./tnbc-living.ts, ./tnbc-treatment.ts);
 * residual disease, capecitabine and olaparib (./tnbc-living.ts); endocrine therapy, ovarian suppression as
 * treatment, CDK4/6 inhibitors and pausing endocrine therapy to try for a baby (./breast-hr-positive.ts);
 * HER2 antibodies and antibody-drug conjugates (./breast-her2-positive.ts); and the immune-related and
 * deruxtecan lung red cards, which stay on the subtype that prescribes those drugs.
 *
 * Three rules were applied throughout. Every claim carries a source. No sentence is a prognosis for one person:
 * where a survival or recurrence figure appears it is quoted with the cohort it came from and the trial that
 * produced it. And because around 420 of the roughly 59,000 breast cancers diagnosed in the UK each year are in
 * men (Cancer Research UK incidence statistics), nothing here says "women"
 * where it means "people"; where a guideline recommendation is itself written about women, the wording is kept as
 * NICE wrote it and the limit is named rather than hidden.
 *
 * Sources, all read 25 September 2026. NICE NG101 (early and locally advanced breast cancer, updated 2025),
 * including section 1.4 surgery, 1.5 reconstruction, 1.13 radiotherapy, 1.14 side effects, lymphoedema, arm and
 * shoulder mobility, menopausal symptoms and bone health, and 1.15 follow-up; NICE NG257 (fertility problems,
 * 2026), section 1.53 fertility preservation for medical indications; NICE NG234 (spinal metastases and metastatic
 * spinal cord compression); NICE CG151 (neutropenic sepsis). The NHS pages on breast cancer, mastectomy,
 * lymphoedema, cellulitis, sepsis, 111 and 999. Macmillan, Cancer Research UK, Breast Cancer Now and Maggie's.
 * And the trials themselves, read through Europe PMC rather than through summaries of them: Veronesi 2002 and
 * Fisher 2002 for the twenty-year comparison of conserving surgery with mastectomy, the EBCTCG 2011 meta-analysis
 * for what radiotherapy after conserving surgery is worth, DiSipio 2013 for the lymphoedema incidence, AMAROS,
 * Z0011, SENOMAC and INSEMA for the axilla, Gartner 2009 for pain and numbness after breast surgery, POEMS and the
 * Lambertini meta-analysis for ovarian protection, and Dilawari 2021 for scalp cooling in Black patients.
 *
 * The cancer record itself (`breast-cancer`) lives in src/data/cancer-parents-wave2.ts. The checklist for the first
 * 60 days is in src/data/first-60-days-checklists.ts, the red cards in src/data/red-flags.ts
 * (cancerIds: ["breast-cancer"]) and the decision aid in src/data/decision-tools/breast-surgery-choice.ts.
 */
